# Contract: Specification Maintenance Procedures (MAINTENANCE.md)

**Location**: `.github/specs/MAINTENANCE.md`
**Purpose**: Documented procedures for maintaining speckit folder structure and governance processes
**Audience**: Governance team, specification maintainers, stakeholders
**Version**: 1.0

## Interface Specification

### File Format & Structure

**Format**: GitHub Markdown (CommonMark) with plain-language procedures

**Required Sections**:

```markdown
# Specification Maintenance Procedures

## Overview
[2-3 sentences describing scope and purpose]

## Numbering Scheme

### How Numbers Are Assigned
- Current specifications: 001-012 (preserved as-is, no renumbering)
- New specifications: Start at 013, increment by 1 (next = highest current + 1)
- Example: If spec 013 exists, next spec is 014
- Rationale: Preserves historical traceability, enables predictable numbering

### Number Reuse
- ❌ Number reuse is NOT allowed after a spec is archived
- Reason: Maintain chronological traceability (who can tell if "spec 003" is original or repurposed?)

## Creating a New Specification

### Prerequisites
- Specification scope is clear (what problem does it solve?)
- Stakeholder need is documented (why is this spec needed?)

### Step-by-Step Process

1. **Determine next number**
   - List all existing specification directories in `.github/specs/`
   - Find highest number (e.g., if 013 exists, next is 014)
   - Confirm number not already assigned

2. **Create directory structure**
   ```bash
   mkdir -p .github/specs/NNN-{slug}
   touch .github/specs/NNN-{slug}/spec.md
   mkdir -p .github/specs/NNN-{slug}/checklists
   ```
   - NNN = 3-digit number (zero-padded: 001, 002, ..., 013)
   - {slug} = lowercase, hyphens, no spaces/special-chars

3. **Use SpecKit workflow**
   - Run `/speckit-specify` with feature description
   - Complete `/speckit-clarify` to resolve ambiguities
   - Complete `/speckit-plan` to design technical approach
   - Complete `/speckit-tasks` to decompose into implementation tasks

4. **Update CATALOG.md**
   - Add row to index table with new number, title, purpose, status, date
   - Add detailed entry section below (see CATALOG.md contract)
   - Update date to creation date

5. **Approval gate**
   - New specification requires review by @ashley (governance authority)
   - Specification must pass all 8 quality dimensions (Constitution Principle VII)
   - Quality checklist in `checklists/requirements.md` must show all items passing

### Acceptance Checklist

- [ ] Directory created as `.github/specs/NNN-{slug}/`
- [ ] spec.md file exists and passes quality checklist
- [ ] checklists/ directory exists with requirements.md
- [ ] Specification number is sequential (no gaps)
- [ ] Folder name slug matches specification title (lowercase, hyphens)
- [ ] CATALOG.md updated with new entry within 7 days
- [ ] @ashley has reviewed and approved specification
- [ ] All 8 quality dimensions passing (Completeness, Clarity, Consistency, Measurability, Scenario Coverage, Edge Cases, Dependencies, Ambiguities)

---

## Updating an Existing Specification

### When to Update
- Clarification needed from stakeholders
- Ambiguity discovered during planning/implementation
- Scope change required
- Quality issues identified in audit

### Update Process

1. **Open change request issue**
   - Title: "[SPEC-UPDATE] NNN - [specific change needed]"
   - Describe: What changed and why
   - Link: Reference original specification number

2. **Update specification**
   - Edit `.github/specs/NNN-{slug}/spec.md`
   - Document change in Clarifications section (if applicable)
   - Re-run `/speckit-clarify` if ambiguity introduced
   - Update `updated_date` in specification metadata

3. **Update quality checklist**
   - Review `checklists/requirements.md` against updated spec
   - Ensure all quality dimensions still passing
   - Mark any items unchecked that are now unmet

4. **Update CATALOG.md**
   - Update `Updated` date for affected entry
   - Update status if changed (Draft → Active or Active → Archived)

5. **Approval gate**
   - Changes require @ashley review (governance authority)
   - Quality checklist must pass all items
   - Changelog entry must document what changed

### Acceptance Checklist

- [ ] Change is documented with rationale
- [ ] spec.md updated with changes clearly marked
- [ ] Quality checklist re-validated (all items passing)
- [ ] CATALOG.md updated with new date and status (if applicable)
- [ ] @ashley has reviewed and approved changes
- [ ] Updated date reflects actual update time

---

## Archiving a Specification

### When to Archive
- Specification is obsolete or superseded
- Specification requires complete rewrite
- Specification scope is no longer relevant

### Archive Process

1. **Mark as archived**
   - Change `status` in spec.md from "Active" to "Archived"
   - Add note: "Archived on [DATE]; see spec [NNN] for replacement" (if applicable)

2. **Preserve in catalog**
   - Move entry to "Archive" section in CATALOG.md (if created)
   - Keep full link and metadata for historical reference
   - ❌ Do NOT delete specification or remove from history

3. **Update references**
   - Update any CLAUDE.md or README links that pointed to archived spec
   - Link to replacement specification (if new one created)

4. **Governance tracking**
   - Document in CHANGELOG.md: why spec was archived, what replaced it

### Acceptance Checklist

- [ ] Archived specification remains in `.github/specs/` (not deleted)
- [ ] spec.md marked as `status: Archived`
- [ ] CATALOG.md entry moved to Archive section
- [ ] Historical links still valid and point to archived spec
- [ ] Replacement specification (if any) is documented

---

## Maintaining CATALOG.md

### Update Frequency
- **Automatic**: Within 7 days of any new specification creation (SC-008)
- **Triggered**: Any time specification status changes (Draft → Clarified, Active → Complete, etc.)
- **Manual check**: Monthly review to ensure all entries are current

### Checklist for CATALOG.md Updates

When updating catalog entries, verify:

- [ ] All specification directories in `.github/specs/` have catalog entry
- [ ] Numbers are sequential with no gaps
- [ ] Status matches spec file status
- [ ] Dates are accurate (created ≤7 days after spec file creation)
- [ ] Links are valid and point to existing directories
- [ ] Index table is sorted by number (ascending: 001, 002, ..., 013)
- [ ] Detailed entries follow same order as table

---

## Quality Gate Enforcement

### Before Specification Becomes Normative

Every specification must pass all 8 quality dimensions before it can be used as normative governance guidance:

1. **Completeness** — All requirements present, all stories complete
2. **Clarity** — Specific, unambiguous, vague terms quantified
3. **Consistency** — Requirements aligned, terminology consistent
4. **Measurability** — Objective acceptance criteria with metrics
5. **Scenario Coverage** — All user flows addressed
6. **Edge Cases** — Boundary conditions defined
7. **Dependencies** — Assumptions documented
8. **Ambiguities** — No unresolved NEEDS CLARIFICATION markers

### Quality Checklist Location
- File: `.github/specs/NNN-{slug}/checklists/requirements.md`
- All items must be checked ([ ] → [x]) before approval

### Failure Action
- If quality checklist has unchecked items → Specification blocked from approval
- Specification author must fix gaps, re-run `/speckit-clarify` if needed
- @ashley reviews updated quality checklist before final approval

---

## Governance Authority

**Specification Approval Authority**: @ashley (organisation owner)

**Decisions Requiring @ashley Approval**:
- ✅ New specification creation
- ✅ Significant specification updates
- ✅ Specification archival/deprecation
- ✅ Changes to numbering scheme or maintenance procedures
- ✅ Quality gate exceptions (rare; requires documented justification)

**Self-Service Decisions** (no approval needed):
- ✅ Minor clarifications or wording fixes
- ✅ CATALOG.md entry updates (status, dates) for existing specs
- ✅ Bug fixes or corrections to implementation tasks

---

## Tools & Commands

### SpecKit Workflow Commands

| Phase | Command | Purpose |
|-------|---------|---------|
| Specify | `/speckit-specify` | Create new specification from user description |
| Clarify | `/speckit-clarify` | Resolve ambiguities with Q&A |
| Plan | `/speckit-plan` | Design technical approach |
| Tasks | `/speckit-tasks` | Decompose into implementation tasks |

### Filesystem Audit Commands

```bash
# List all specification directories
ls -d .github/specs/[0-9][0-9][0-9]-*/

# Count existing specifications
ls -d .github/specs/[0-9][0-9][0-9]-*/ | wc -l

# Find highest number
ls -d .github/specs/[0-9][0-9][0-9]-*/ | sed 's/.*\///' | cut -d- -f1 | sort -n | tail -1

# Verify spec.md exists in all directories
for dir in .github/specs/[0-9][0-9][0-9]-*/; do [ ! -f "$dir/spec.md" ] && echo "Missing: $dir"; done
```

---

## Examples

### Example: Creating Specification 014

```bash
# Step 1: Determine next number
# Current highest: 013, so next is 014

# Step 2: Create directory
mkdir -p .github/specs/014-example-feature
mkdir -p .github/specs/014-example-feature/checklists

# Step 3: Run SpecKit
/speckit-specify "Example feature that demonstrates SpecKit workflow"

# Step 4: Clarify and plan
/speckit-clarify
/speckit-plan

# Step 5: Update CATALOG.md
# Add row to index table:
# | 014 | Example Feature | Demonstrates SpecKit workflow | Draft | 2026-09-17 | [spec/link](./014-example-feature/spec.md) |

# Add detailed entry:
### 014 - Example Feature
Example feature specification...
```

